In July 2026, Moonshot AI, based&nbsp;
in Beijing, released Kimi K3.&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:14.160
It was open source, had near-frontier performance,&nbsp;
and was very cheap. And everyone panicked. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.480
the AI stocks dropped, and this was actually&nbsp;
when NVIDIA dropped from its spot as the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.560
valuable company. OpenAI and Anthropic accused&nbsp;
Chinese labs of copying their models, and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.880
were even lobbying Washington to restrict them.&nbsp;
But Huang, said the opposite. Which seems weird.&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.400
This also led to Huang’s first-ever post on X&nbsp;
being an open letter, all about open models.&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.920
“For my first post, I’m sharing a letter NVIDIA&nbsp;
signed on why open models matter. Open models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.600
strengthen safety and cybersecurity, accelerate&nbsp;
innovation and diffusion, and enable sovereignty.”&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.480
“Open weights give customers greater&nbsp;
control. As organizations invest in AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.560
they want to know that they will not become&nbsp;
locked into a single provider or lose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.680
knowledge and capabilities they build over time.”
And it’s true that open-source software changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:08.000
the world. Just look at Linux, an open-source&nbsp;
OS. “90% of all public cloud infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:16.560
globally runs on” Linux. 91% of Google Cloud,&nbsp;
83% of AWS, and 61% of Microsoft Azure.&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.960
So, why are Huang and NVIDIA, so passionate about&nbsp;
these open models? Why do this, if your biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:29.120
customers, are those running proprietary AI&nbsp;
models? Wouldn’t this hurt NVIDIA’s revenue? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.000
actually, it could be the opposite.
It’s in an interview with Axios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.160
that we get an even clearer picture.
“There's no scenario where China runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.600
U.S. companies off the road. Zero possibility.&nbsp;
The market misunderstood the impact of DeepSeek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.560
the first time, [and] misunderstood the impact of&nbsp;
Kimi again this time. Free AI should be great for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.760
hardware. Free AI should be great for chips.&nbsp;
Free AI should be great for data centers.”&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.040
His argument, is that openness makes AI more&nbsp;
secure, as researchers inspect the models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.320
expose weaknesses and build defences. But I&nbsp;
think the real reason is that more models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.240
means more business for NVIDIA. Every AI&nbsp;
model, free or paid, has to run on chips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:16.000
When the model is free, more people use AI,&nbsp;
and every one of those uses runs on compute.&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.440
More data centre revenue. NVIDIA made $89.0&nbsp;
billion from data centres last quarter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:28.960
up 117% year on year, at a 75% gross margin.
But it also means more demand for NVIDIA chips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:34.560
and GPUs, if someone’s running a model locally.&nbsp;
In NVIDIA's September 2026 regulatory filing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.520
they said “Demand for open-source foundation&nbsp;
models and applications based on them promotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.800
the use of our products worldwide”.
Okay, that explains why open models. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:50.640
why spend $26 billion building your own? There&nbsp;
are already tons of companies building them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.040
It seems like a terrible idea, especially when&nbsp;
it means going head to head with the companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:07.440
buying your chips… like OpenAI. Unless&nbsp;
you've pulled this exact trick before.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:13.120
NVIDIA won the GPU war, not just with chips,&nbsp;
but with the free infrastructure around it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:19.200
CUDA. NVIDIA was way ahead of their time with&nbsp;
this, and it let programmers use GPUs for general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:25.760
computing beyond graphics. CUDA was free and&nbsp;
robust, and became the standard… but it only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.640
ran on NVIDIA chips. It made switching&nbsp;
to a rival chip extremely difficult.&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:37.120
Nemotron is the same thing, just one layer&nbsp;
up: Free models, tuned for NVIDIA hardware.&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.760
Those who use Nemotron and related&nbsp;
models stay within the NVIDIA ecosystem.&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.360
NVIDIA wants whoever wins the model race to run&nbsp;
on NVIDIA. So, they invest in state-of-the-art AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.480
models. You can download them for free, and run&nbsp;
them totally locally, without a subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.960
and without anything sent to the cloud… or OpenAI.
A company can build an app on top of Nemotron and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.760
fine-tune it on its own data, or, someone&nbsp;
can run a personal AI with Nemotron Nano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:10.080
on a single GPU in a laptop or desktop. And&nbsp;
all of this, puts OpenAI in a difficult spot.&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.760
In August 2026, in NVIDIA’s&nbsp;
financial results press release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.720
Huang said that “This time last year, one&nbsp;
lab alone was driving the buildout; today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.080
we have a golden age of new AI labs and startups,&nbsp;
multiple frontier labs scaling in parallel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.920
a thriving open-model ecosystem&nbsp;
and physical AI coming online.“&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:34.400
He doesn’t name anyone, but he’s saying that&nbsp;
NVIDIA doesn’t depend on OpenAI as much, aka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:40.720
that “one lab alone”. But ironically, OpenAI&nbsp;
is doing something very similar to NVIDIA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:45.120
which explains the strange case when&nbsp;
OpenAI personally annoyed Huang.&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:51.040
OpenAI has a problem. It’s made about a&nbsp;
trillion dollars in purchase commitments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:57.200
but ironically, it owns one of the least&nbsp;
valuable parts of the whole AI equation: Models.&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.640
You might say “How? They have about a&nbsp;
billion monthly users!” And that’s true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.560
but that doesn’t actually matter, if that layer is&nbsp;
easy to substitute. AI models are getting easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:14.560
to substitute, but GPUs and compute are still&nbsp;
extremely valuable, and very difficult to replace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:20.000
AI models, in a way, are becoming commodities.
Someone actually laid a lot of this out almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.840
25 years ago, and weirdly, predicted&nbsp;
this exact situation. In 2002, in Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:31.200
Spolsky’s essay “Strategy Letter V”, he says:
“Every product in the marketplace has substitutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.840
and complements. A substitute is another&nbsp;
product you might buy if the first product is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:40.960
too expensive. Smart companies try to commoditize&nbsp;
their products’ complements. If you can do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:45.440
demand for your product will increase and you&nbsp;
will be able to charge more and make more. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.400
IBM designed the PC architecture, they used&nbsp;
off-the-shelf parts instead of custom parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:55.200
Within a short time scrillions of companies&nbsp;
sprung up offering memory cards, hard drives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:00.400
graphics cards, printers, etc. Cheap&nbsp;
add-ins meant more demand for PCs.”&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.560
He also wrote “why don’t the video chip vendors&nbsp;
of the world try to commoditize the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.040
somehow? That’ s a lot harder. If&nbsp;
the game Halo is selling like crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.840
it doesn’t really have any substitutes.”
Well, you might not be able to substitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:20.160
video games, but you can substitute AI models.&nbsp;
So OpenAI’s supplier and investor is funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.520
free models that compete with what it sells.&nbsp;
By doing so, it increases demand for NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.640
chips. Even if proprietary models have better&nbsp;
performance, this is still eroding value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.760
and is a huge problem for OpenAI.
So… why don’t they just do the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:43.760
but in reverse? On June 24, 2026, OpenAI unveiled&nbsp;
Jalapeño, its first chip, designed with Broadcom.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:47.600
So, today we are announcing a partnership&nbsp;
between Broadcom and OpenAI. We've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:51.280
working together for about the last 18&nbsp;
months designing a new custom chip. Uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:54.480
more recently we've also started working&nbsp;
on a whole custom system. These things have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:59.200
gotten so complex you need the whole thing.&nbsp;
Uh, this is a full system, so we worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.400
we've closely calibrated for a while on designing&nbsp;
a chip that is specific for our workloads, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:09.280
it became clear to us just how much capacity,&nbsp;
inference capacity, what was going to need.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:13.600
They also wrote: “OpenAI is not only&nbsp;
developing frontier models or building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:18.400
products on top of them; it is designing&nbsp;
the infrastructure underneath them.”

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.800
We have a deep understanding of the workload,&nbsp;
and we work with a number of parties across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:28.320
the ecosystem. And there's a number of chips&nbsp;
out there that I think are really incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.280
um, and that there's a niche for each one. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:35.680
we've really been looking like for specific&nbsp;
workloads where we feel they are underserved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.760
How can we build something that will&nbsp;
be able to accelerate what's possible?

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:45.520
OpenAI models can be more efficient this way, and&nbsp;
reduce the gigantic margin NVIDIA is taking. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.640
I think it’s about more than just efficiency.&nbsp;
It’s also to protect their part of the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:56.400
stack. Making more compute options available&nbsp;
for AI, and making their own models better.&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.120
It was also reported that OpenAI was&nbsp;
unsatisfied with some of NVIDIA’s latest chips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:05.360
and was looking for alternatives. OpenAI&nbsp;
also said it would use chips from startup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.560
Cerebras in a deal worth over $10 billion,&nbsp;
which is a direct move away from NVIDIA.&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:15.680
OpenAI isn’t the only ones, though. Google&nbsp;
has made its own AI chips, called TPUs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:20.480
for about a decade and now rents and sells them&nbsp;
to other companies, like Anthropic. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:25.280
also in talks to supply Meta, and Google Cloud&nbsp;
executives estimated that deal alone could equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:31.440
up to 10% of NVIDIA's data centre business.
Amazon also builds its own Trainium chips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:37.360
Wonder what those are for? Microsoft&nbsp;
launched its Maia 200 chip in January 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:45.200
built to run AI models including OpenAI's GPT-5.2.
But, OpenAI had a problem still. They needed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:51.040
way to increase use and reliance, on their chips.&nbsp;
One answer, was the strange case of Hugging Face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:58.640
Where an AI hacking into their systems, weirdly&nbsp;
led to an investment opportunity between the two.&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:09.520
If you’ve watched this far, you probably like&nbsp;
our stuff, so consider subscribing! It helps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.880
channel and means you won’t miss future videos.
To understand why Hugging Face is important here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.440
why OpenAI offered to invest $100 million, and&nbsp;
why NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.800
many times that… What the heck is Hugging Face?
Simply put, it’s a website where people share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.720
AI models, for free. Anyone can&nbsp;
download one, run it on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:34.080
own computer, and even build a product on it.
It’s quickly become the main place the AI world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:39.920
finds, tests, and shares models, with 18 million&nbsp;
developers and 3 million models on it. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:45.360
what’s important, is that it also sells you&nbsp;
the option to run models on its own servers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.840
Which brings us to a strange series of&nbsp;
events, and a fight over Hugging Face.&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:57.200
In July 2026, OpenAI was testing its models on a&nbsp;
hacking benchmark called ExploitGym. Two of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.520
escaped. Well, “escaped” might not be the right&nbsp;
word. They were given a test benchmark, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.840
two, GPT-5.6 Sol and another unreleased model,&nbsp;
broke out of their test environment, got onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:14.800
internet, and hacked into Hugging Face's systems.
Why? Well… to find the benchmark's answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:19.760
key so they could cheat on the&nbsp;
test. They found it, by the way.&nbsp;

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:24.400
Hugging Face caught it first, then OpenAI,&nbsp;
and the two released a joint statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:29.680
Hugging Face's CEO said there was "no malicious&nbsp;
intent" from OpenAI and called it "mind-blowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:35.120
that all of this happened autonomously”.
I know this is a bit unsettling, but I do find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:40.320
it a bit funny that the reason they broke out, was&nbsp;
to cheat on a test. Like a kid hacking into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:46.080
Pentagon, to get answers for their school quiz.
Regardless, afterwards came some surprising news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.320
OpenAI offered to invest $100 million&nbsp;
into Hugging Face. Investment talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.840
were actually triggered by this incident.&nbsp;
Why? Well, remember what I said earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.720
about Hugging Face running models on servers?
OpenAI envisioned using Hugging Face as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:05.520
outlet for its custom Jalapeño chips. This&nbsp;
actually, seemed to annoy Jensen Huang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:11.040
who “privately complained about their move&nbsp;
into semiconductors.” And I can see why: it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:16.960
their biggest customer reducing reliance on just&nbsp;
NVIDIA, which puts part of their business at risk.&nbsp;

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:23.760
But the talks between OpenAI and Hugging Face,&nbsp;
soon fell apart, and then… in came NVIDIA. Nvidia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:29.120
signed a deal to buy Hugging Face for $12.9&nbsp;
billion. Hugging Face had turned down a $500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:34.000
million NVIDIA investment a year earlier.
So, why is NVIDIA buying Hugging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.160
Face? Open-source AI.
This was actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:43.360
filing where NVIDIA said “Demand for open-source&nbsp;
foundation models and applications based on them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:49.200
promotes the use of our products worldwide”.
So, OpenAI wanted the world's open-model hub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.720
to be a way to increase use and&nbsp;
leverage for its own chips. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:57.920
it’ll soon be owned by NVIDIA, who are using&nbsp;
it to increase use of their open-source models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:03.040
and thus, increase reliance on their chips.
And, here’s some more interesting stats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:08.320
NVIDIA is the largest contributor of&nbsp;
open models and data to Hugging Face.&nbsp;

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:14.400
There’s a massive shakeup happening in AI. It&nbsp;
seems AI models are becoming commodities. Easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:20.080
to switch between, cheaper to run, with fewer&nbsp;
barriers to entry. So now, players like OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:25.280
and NVIDIA, are trying their best to remain&nbsp;
valuable, un-substitutable parts of the stack.&nbsp;

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:30.720
If anything, this shows us that the AI future&nbsp;
likely won’t be in the hands of Anthropic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:36.240
OpenAI. But with open-source models, running&nbsp;
either in massive data centres or on your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:43.360
GPU at home. Hopefully this means AI&nbsp;
is safer and more secure. We’ll see.&nbsp;

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:50.560
There’s one silent part of all this: Broadcom. Big&nbsp;
winners of AI right now, but, they did something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:55.840
else that no one seemed to notice. They acquired&nbsp;
one of the most valuable pieces of software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:00.800
on earth. One that runs things from banks, to&nbsp;
grocery stores, to government infrastructure.&nbsp;

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:06.320
But afterwards, most customers saw&nbsp;
their costs jump up to 1,000%. Broadcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:10.640
is quietly holding the world hostage.&nbsp;
